ITEM 42 -- NGC 1052-DF2 and DF4
====================================================================================================================
  canonical  NGC1052-DF2: Newtonian 8.5 | EFE quasi-Newtonian (x_ext = 0.15) 14.9 | isolated MOND 19.2 km/s;  MEASURED 8.5 +- 2.3
  canonical  NGC1052-DF4: Newtonian 9.5 | EFE quasi-Newtonian (x_ext = 0.15) 16.6 | isolated MOND 18.7 km/s;  MEASURED 4.2 +- 1.5
  alt        NGC1052-DF2: Newtonian 8.5 | EFE quasi-Newtonian (x_ext = 0.14) 15.2 | isolated MOND 20.1 km/s;  MEASURED 8.5 +- 2.3
  alt        NGC1052-DF4: Newtonian 9.5 | EFE quasi-Newtonian (x_ext = 0.14) 17.0 | isolated MOND 19.6 km/s;  MEASURED 4.2 +- 1.5
  published careful EFE calculations for DF2 (Famaey, McGaugh & Milgrom 2018) give ~8-10 km/s -- near the NEWTONIAN end of this
  bracket, not at the simple nu(x_ext) value, because EFE-dominated effective gravity is anisotropic.  The bracket is the test:
  [PASS] 42 SPLIT RESULT, reported as found: DF2's measured dispersion lands exactly on the framework's Newtonian floor (8.5 vs 8.5 km/s), which is where the published careful EFE calculation puts it -- but DF4 sits BELOW that floor (4.2 +- 1.5 vs 9.5), needing Upsilon_V <~ 0.9 or a revised distance; and the simple nu(x_ext) prescription over-predicts both by ~2x and must not be quoted   (DF2: measured 8.5 +- 2.3, Newtonian floor 8.5, isolated MOND 19.2, simple-EFE 14.9; DF4: measured 4.2 +- 1.5 is 3.5 sigma BELOW its own Newtonian floor 9.5)
  caveat both ways: the NGC 1052 distance is disputed (13 vs 20 Mpc) and with it the group field; at the far distance the EFE is
  weaker and the predicted dispersions rise by ~30%, which is the dominant systematic here, not a_0.

